e doubt  in the case of crimes against humanity  of course we're not dealing with  isolated or random war crimes but a  widespread or systematic attack against  the civilian population  so there has to be a significant volume  of evidence which shows that  these are in fact  massive crimes that are being committed  not just at a specific location at a  specific time  but really spread across a time and a  wider geographic space  quick last question since russia is not  a member of the court and we should  point out neither is the united states  it has no legal duty then to turn over  its people for prosecution so could  vladimir putin if enough evidence is  found to convict him ever face any  punishment  well it's a matter of speculation uh who  would be eventually the target of an  investigation  a prosecutor will typically go where the  evidence leads  but once arrest warrants are issued uh  the estate that is not a party to the  statute of the court may have no  obligation to cooperate but then that  person cannot step foot outside of that  country uh in particular in european  countries that are all members  of the international criminal court  because there they will face arrest  right professor akavan thank you thank  you  next an afghan family flees a second war  their struggle to get out of ukraine  among the exodus of people from ukraine  are some who don't call the country home  there are foreign students and workers  who found themselves caught in the  conflict and there's at least one family  who fled the war in afghanistan hoping  to make it to a new life in canada none  of their journeys have been easy redmond  shannon reports  afghan refugee mir safi spoke with  global news earlier this week as his  seven family members including a newborn  baby slept on the floor of a ukrainian  school near the polish border safi says  they were blocked from leaving ukraine  four times in the past week there have  been many reports of non-ukrainians  facing discrimin