ssible  war crimes in ukraine after receiving  requests from 39 countries including  canada an advanced team has left the  hague for the ukraine region  moving so fast is unprecedented for the  court to help us understand the  implications i'm joined by payam akkavan  a professor of international law at the  university of toronto and a former u.n  prosecutor professor why do you think  the head prosecutor is moving so fast  well the  invasion of ukraine  is a humanitarian disaster of  unprecedented dimensions and  while the mandate of the prosecutor of  the international criminal court is to  investigate and prosecute international  crimes  um one aspect of that mandate is  deterrence basically to try and deter  ongoing violations so while there  appears to be  evidence that war crimes and possibly  crimes against humanity uh are being  committed uh it's important also to  signal to the perpetrators that  the world is watching that their conduct  is going to be scrutinized and that  there may be a price to pay there have  been many reports already of attacks on  civilians in ukraine is an intentional  attack on civilians a war crime  well most certainly that is  the  most emphatic war crime  but also indiscriminate attacks are  equally war crimes if you're using  weapons or methods of warfare that fail  to distinguish  between military targets and civilian  population that is also  a war crime and of course  the burden of proof is uh more  exacting  for establishing intentional attacks  against civilian populations but it's  enough to show that the attacks were  indiscriminate  about the burden of proof what is the  threshold for laying charges of a war  crime or a crime against humanity what  evidence will the investigators be  looking for will they be interviewing  witnesses  well yes the  witness testimony is always central to  establishing such a case  physical evidence video footage  there are multiple sources that go to  establishing a case  beyond a reasonable doubt  in the case of crimes against humanity  of course we're not dealing with  isolated or random war crimes but a  widespread or systematic attack against  the civilian population  so there has to be a significant volume  of evidence which shows that  these are in fact  massive crimes that are being committed  not just at a specific location at a  specific time  but really spread across a time and a  wider geographic space  quick last question since russia is not  a member of the court and we should  point out neither is the united states  it has no legal duty then to turn over  its people for prosecution so could  vladimir putin if enough evidence is  found to convict him ever face any  punishment  well it's a matter of speculation uh who  would be eventually the target of an  investigation  a prosecutor will typically go where the  evidence leads  but once arrest warrants are issued uh  the estate that is not a party to the  statute of the court may have no  obligation to cooperate but then that  person cannot step foot outside of that  country uh in particular in european  countries that are all members  of the international criminal court  because there they will face arrest  right professor akavan thank you thank  you  next an afghan family flees a second war  their struggle to get out of ukraine  among the exodus of people from ukraine  are some who don't call the country home  there are foreign students and workers  who found themselves caught in the  conflict and there's at least one family  who fled the war in afghanistan hoping  to make it to a new life in canada none  of their journeys have been easy redmond  shannon reports  afghan refugee mir safi spoke with  global news earlier this week as his  seven family members including a newborn  baby slept on the floor of a ukrainian  school near the polish border safi says  they were blocked from leaving ukraine  four times in the past week there have  been many reports of non-ukrainians  facing discrimination while trying to  leave particularly visible minorities  it's very frustrating to see ukrainians  cross the border without being asked to  stop but me as an afghan they stopped me  and they say my baby daughter does not  have papers the safy's daughter sumiya  was born one day before the invasion  this was all the ukrainian border  authorities they didn't even give me  time to speak the safes finally made it  out wednesday night and are now in  germany  for the saffies this is the second  traumatic departure in six months they  fled the taliban last august and applied  to come to canada hoping to have been  approved long before now will the  minister look into this case and help  this family and others like them come to  canada expeditiously we can't discuss  the specifics of any case i'm happy to  look into whatever sensitive cases  may come across our desk  just like the hawk malls whom global  news spoke with in february the safies  are unsure when their case will be  processed two families praying to find  peace caught up in two brutal conflicts  redmond shannon global news london  there are so many individual stories in  this exodus tonight we leave you with  what's happening at the central train  station in berlin germany  thousands of people showed up to meet  trains filled with refugees from ukraine  who fled with almost nothing berliners  are opening their homes holding up signs  offering rooms and  opening their hearts  we cannot just sit at home and just look  at the news on the right side there is  like thousands of people from berlin  offering place to stay this is  absolutely incredible  food medical aid guidance and friendship  a safe haven and a sign of this war's  immense and continuing cost  thanks for watching  you
 

